h do we have  time and money to  come back uh and build this  so i suppose the question is touching on  really to what extent are pledges  genuine to what extent are they green  washing who's even keeping track brief  word from the olynyk i have no idea  who's keeping track but i am  fairly confident that there's an awful  lot of green washing going on i was  fascinated by the  the graphic that was put up earlier  about the the way in which companies  have been making promises and have been  committing to things and can be shown to  be doing  taking action and reducing emissions and  that is much less true of um of working  at government level  and that is definitely something that  needs to be taken account of more it's  very easy to talk it's very easy to make  promises and pledges and say we're going  to do these wonderful things we know  that it is harder to do the next step  and nobody's going to say that that's  easy but definitely the steps need to be  taken and it's not enough to come up  with a great big you know plan and a  promise without actually having a  delivery uh a delivery plan behind that  um and the commitments that have been  made in the exhortations that have been  made are insufficient and this is i'm  sure we're going to get onto cop26 but  you know that is the the big issue  around cop26 that so many promises are  going to be made and it's going to be  glitzy and and glamorous and you know on  the tv all the time and people are going  to get credit for great big  announcements but actually there's a lot  of work that needs to be done behind  that and sometimes the glitz and the  glamour and the announcements can  actually uh distract from the fact that  very little is going on we'll come on  we'll finish actually by talking about  our hopes and fears for cop 26. but  actually just want to come back to you  on that very question i mean your pledge  to cut emissions by 30 by 2030 who keeps  track of that  so we we follow a science-based uh  target system where it's audited uh by  accountants as you talk about as well  and i think the key thing for us is some  of our pledges are not as far-reaching  as other people in the industry but we  have had years of taking action and  delivering on our promises and what  that's taught us  is actually it's very important to make  far-reaching commitments so we think  that's important because it it talks  about the aspiration the leadership  behind it but it's how you follow up on  it so i think measurement is really  important commitment at operational  level  as well as at leadership level and then  being very clear on the programs that  drive improvement whether it's on farm  in factory or actually in the way that  we move products  so actually for for us we spend much  more energy and how do we bring things  to life in reality  than the pledges uh that we make  and the way that we do that is in the  way that we follow up on data  so our data collection not only on on  farm for instance is is annual and and  every year we publish  uh in a transparent way the summary of  all of those results but what we also do  is we individually share that with our  farmers  so in an anonymized way that they can  then see how they compare to the average  farmer and so on and see where they can  improve so for me it's very much about  being able to measure have programs just  one thing that diane said that i thought  was a really really important point to  pick up on and the role of government as  well  it's quite often in the early days there  is that balance in terms of cost versus  investment and does it pay off but that  can often uh be improved by being able  to scale the idea so i think for me  scaling is also really important and  therefore government can really  play a hand there in terms of  inter-industry collaboration to be able  to really scale some of these ideas so  they aren't an economic kind of negative  uh for people to make the the  investments  really interesti