t really there so  it just takes too long to make those  decisions and too little money is  actually being spent and if you just  look at what is required between now and  2030 of course cop 26 is going to be  looking at the ultimate you know  temperature increase and really you know  looking at all the evidence science that  we've just been talking about um which  is saying that we we're nearly there  already in terms of the 1.5  centigrade increase and we've already  are at 1.1 nothing 1.2 so there really  isn't very much time so it's crucial  what happens between now and 2030 and  that amount of money is now being  recalculated in terms of what is needed  so  just the the european union is saying  that what they need to be spending is  360 billion a year between now and 2030  and 125 billion  of uh digitalization which is absolutely  essential now you know i mentioned  earlier that they're putting out the  first bond for 20 billion well that's  very very little by comparison to what  is actually needed and i think it's the  ambition that has to be there and has to  be if you like measured and monitored um  in terms of how they do from here on it  starts the ball rolling  but it shouldn't start the ball rolling  it's it should just push it further i  mean the bulls should really have  started  a long time ago yeah yeah and uh word  from you lindy about what you're hoping  to see  it's a great opportunity here in the uk  that um that glasgow is the host for cop  26 and um and i know that there's plenty  of opportunity for the uk to be making  its own promises and announcements a  huge concern though and it links with  with um  uh with something that both vicky and um  and diane said is that at the same time  we are in the uk have not  um  we are still looking at developing a  massive oil field west of shetland the  cambo oil field and this will be  equivalent in its emissions to 18 coal  fired power stations it's also going to  be vastly expensive so um blackrock who  you mentioned is having you know  billions of  dollars available for investment are one  of the key players in thinking that they  will invest within cambo  um so you have a bit of a perfect storm  there of lots of money that could be  spent in other ways being spent on  uh exploiting fossil fuels now the  government have the opportunity to say  no this camp this can't happen we are  not going to allow the exploitation in  that way because at the moment there's a  huge degree of hypocrisy in insisting on  other countries doing their bit when  we're not taking those sort of stands  ourselves there is money available both  in the private sector and indeed the  public sector because as diane pointed  out we are um just about the the the  country with the most favorable regime  in terms of uh  tax credits and an actual um  funding support uh and subsidies for  fossil fuels  and lots of oil and gas companies want  to be based here but we need to address  that because the money is there to do  the sort of things which are needed to  be done  and they need to be done very quickly so  we're hoping that the uk will take a  stand that  diana had wanted to come to you for a  final word from you about what you hope  to get out of cop 26 but i'm being told  that we are all out of time i'm afraid  unless you could do it in one sentence  so yes repurposing oil and gas workers  through a series of events that we're  doing at malin which is just opposite  cop26 where we're bringing the whole  shipping industry together  to create an action plan through  the  oil and gas sector transferring their  knowledge and expertise into green power  for shipping that's what we want to do  at clock26 and that's what we're doing  okay all right we are out of time i hope  you enjoyed that panel discussion got  through as much as we could there  there's so much obviously that we could  still talk about i'm going to give you  all a 15-minute comfort break now and  when you come back trevor phill